<PubmedArticleSet>
<PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">15521160</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2004</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>09</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2026</Year><Month>01</Month><Day>28</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Print">1088-0224</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Print"><Volume>10</Volume><Issue>10</Issue><PubDate><Year>2004</Year><Month>Oct</Month></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>The American journal of managed care</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Am J Manag Care</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis: a primary care consensus guideline.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>689</StartPage><EndPage>696</EndPage><MedlinePgn>689-96</MedlinePgn></Pagination><Abstract><AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To develop consensus on appropriate treatment for acute exacerbation of chronic bronchitis (AECB). CHARACTERISTICS AND ETIOLOGY: Patients with chronic bronchitis have an irreversible reduction in maximal airflow velocity and a productive cough on most days of the month for 3 months over 2 consecutive years. An AECB is characterized by a period of unstable lung function with worsening airflow and other symptoms. Most (80%) cases of AECB are due to infection, with half due to aerobic bacteria. The remaining 20% are due to noninfectious causes such as environmental factors or medication nonadherence.</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Supportive care should be provided to all patients, which might include removal of irritants, use of a bronchodilator, oxygen, hydration, use of a systemic corticosteroid, and chest physical therapy. Antibacterial treatment should be reserved for patients with at least 1 key symptom (ie, increased dyspnea, sputum production, sputum purulence) and 1 risk factor (ie, age &gt; or = 65 years, forced expiratory volume in 1 second &lt; 50% of the predicted value, &gt; or = 4 AECBs in 12 months, 1 or more comorbidities). A newer macrolide, extended-spectrum cephalosporin, or doxycycline is appropriate for an exacerbation of moderate severity, and high-dose amoxicillin/clavulanate or a respiratory fluoroquinolone should be used for a severe exacerbation. There has been increasing antibacterial resistance by the 3 most prevalent pathogens (Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Moraxella catarrhalis).</AbstractText><AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Although all AECB patients should receive supportive care, only patients with at least 1 key symptom and 1 risk factor should receive antibiotic therapy.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Brunton</LastName><ForeName>Stephen</ForeName><Initials>S</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Family Medicine, University of California, Irvine, Calif, USA.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Carmichael</LastName><ForeName>Blaine P</ForeName><Initials>BP</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Colgan</LastName><ForeName>Richard</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Feeney</LastName><ForeName>A Susan</ForeName><Initials>AS</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Fendrick</LastName><ForeName>A Mark</ForeName><Initials>AM</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Quintiliani</LastName><ForeName>Richard</ForeName><Initials>R</Initials></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Scott</LastName><ForeName>Gregory</ForeName><Initials>G</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><PublicationTypeList><PublicationType UI="D016446">Consensus Statement</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016428">Journal Article</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D013485">Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't</PublicationType><PublicationType UI="D016454">Review</PublicationType></PublicationTypeList></Article><Medline